1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,861
A swing drops, and your stomach floats.

2
00:00:02,861 --> 00:00:05,050
A diver feels weightless in the air.

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:07,044
Astronauts float in their station.

4
00:00:07,444 --> 00:00:09,215
Did their weight really change?

5
00:00:09,665 --> 00:00:12,759
Diya takes the hospital
lift up to visit a patient.

6
00:00:12,759 --> 00:00:14,862
As it starts, she feels heavier.

7
00:00:15,212 --> 00:00:18,036
As it stops at the top
floor, she feels lighter.

8
00:00:18,036 --> 00:00:20,414
Did her weight change, or something else?

9
00:00:20,914 --> 00:00:25,120
By the end of this video, you will
know exactly what she feels, with one

10
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:26,067
short formula.

11
00:00:26,717 --> 00:00:28,385
So what is apparent weight?

12
00:00:28,735 --> 00:00:31,313
Your true weight is m g, the Earth's pull.

13
00:00:31,313 --> 00:00:33,391
In the lift, it does not change.

14
00:00:33,791 --> 00:00:35,904
But you never feel gravity directly.

15
00:00:35,904 --> 00:00:38,481
You feel the floor
pushing up on your feet.

16
00:00:38,881 --> 00:00:43,543
In episode sixty, we saw that a scale
reads that push, the normal force

17
00:00:43,543 --> 00:00:44,443
N.

18
00:00:44,555 --> 00:00:46,604
So your apparent weight is N.

19
00:00:46,604 --> 00:00:49,588
And N can change, even when m g does not.

20
00:00:50,238 --> 00:00:51,236
Let's run it.

21
00:00:51,236 --> 00:00:54,819
Diya, fifty kilograms, stands
on a scale in the lift.

22
00:00:55,169 --> 00:00:58,308
At rest, the scale reads fifty kilograms.

23
00:00:58,308 --> 00:00:59,578
N equals m g.

24
00:00:59,978 --> 00:01:03,830
The lift starts up, speeding up
at one metre per second squared.

25
00:01:04,230 --> 00:01:06,804
The scale jumps to fifty five point one.

26
00:01:06,804 --> 00:01:09,718
The floor must push
harder, to speed her up.

27
00:01:10,118 --> 00:01:13,092
Now it rises at a steady
two metres per second.

28
00:01:13,092 --> 00:01:15,973
a is zero, so the
reading is back to fifty.

29
00:01:16,373 --> 00:01:18,764
Near the top floor, it slows down.

30
00:01:18,764 --> 00:01:20,109
Now a points down.

31
00:01:20,509 --> 00:01:22,933
The scale drops to forty four point nine.

32
00:01:22,933 --> 00:01:25,803
She feels lighter, though
she is still going up.

33
00:01:26,303 --> 00:01:28,205
Slow-mo replay of the stop.

34
00:01:28,555 --> 00:01:30,269
The velocity points up.

35
00:01:30,269 --> 00:01:32,297
The acceleration points down.

36
00:01:32,297 --> 00:01:35,935
The scale follows the
acceleration, not the velocity.

37
00:01:36,585 --> 00:01:38,853
Now the formula, step by step.

38
00:01:38,853 --> 00:01:40,382
Take up as positive.

39
00:01:40,732 --> 00:01:41,632
Step one.

40
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:43,308
Two forces act on Diya.

41
00:01:43,308 --> 00:01:45,046
N from the scale, up.

42
00:01:45,046 --> 00:01:47,203
And her weight, m g, down.

43
00:01:47,953 --> 00:01:48,980
Step two.

44
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:50,709
Newton's second law.

45
00:01:50,709 --> 00:01:52,989
N minus m g equals m a.

46
00:01:53,739 --> 00:01:54,717
Step three.

47
00:01:54,717 --> 00:01:57,034
So N equals m times g plus a.

48
00:01:57,034 --> 00:02:01,274
Speeding up going up, a is
up, and N is more than m g.

49
00:02:02,024 --> 00:02:02,984
Step four.

50
00:02:02,984 --> 00:02:06,581
If a points down, N
equals m times g minus a.

51
00:02:06,581 --> 00:02:08,048
She feels lighter.

52
00:02:08,798 --> 00:02:09,901
Step five.

53
00:02:09,901 --> 00:02:12,385
In free fall, a equals g.

54
00:02:12,385 --> 00:02:14,692
N is m times g minus g.

55
00:02:14,692 --> 00:02:15,592
Zero.

56
00:02:16,152 --> 00:02:17,279
Remember this.

57
00:02:17,279 --> 00:02:21,988
A scale reads N, and N follows the
acceleration, not the velocity.

58
00:02:22,688 --> 00:02:24,222
Now, side by side.

59
00:02:24,572 --> 00:02:26,622
Going up, but slowing down.

60
00:02:26,622 --> 00:02:30,324
The acceleration points
down, so N is less than m g.

61
00:02:30,724 --> 00:02:32,736
Going down, and speeding up.

62
00:02:32,736 --> 00:02:34,993
The acceleration points down again.

63
00:02:34,993 --> 00:02:36,043
Lighter again.

64
00:02:36,643 --> 00:02:37,671
Now you try.

65
00:02:37,671 --> 00:02:41,636
The lift goes down, and slows
to stop at the ground floor.

66
00:02:41,636 --> 00:02:43,942
Does Diya feel heavier or lighter?

67
00:02:43,942 --> 00:02:45,220
Pause and decide.

68
00:02:48,421 --> 00:02:49,321
Heavier!

69
00:02:49,201 --> 00:02:54,237
Slowing a downward motion needs an
upward acceleration, so N is more than

70
00:02:54,237 --> 00:02:55,137
m g.

71
00:02:55,514 --> 00:02:57,172
So what is weightlessness?

72
00:02:57,172 --> 00:02:58,357
Follow the links.

73
00:02:58,707 --> 00:02:59,607
One.

74
00:02:59,218 --> 00:03:02,884
A box falls freely, with a
scale and an apple inside.

75
00:03:03,284 --> 00:03:04,184
Two.

76
00:03:03,867 --> 00:03:07,740
Box, scale and apple all
fall with acceleration g.

77
00:03:08,140 --> 00:03:09,040
Three.

78
00:03:08,727 --> 00:03:11,165
So the scale has nothing to push on.

79
00:03:11,165 --> 00:03:13,651
N is zero, and the reading is zero.

80
00:03:14,051 --> 00:03:14,951
Four.

81
00:03:14,569 --> 00:03:16,564
The apple still has its weight.

82
00:03:16,564 --> 00:03:18,846
It just feels nothing holding it up.

83
00:03:18,846 --> 00:03:20,305
That is weightlessness.

84
00:03:20,905 --> 00:03:21,805
One more.

85
00:03:21,690 --> 00:03:25,625
In a space station, does gravity
still pull on the astronauts?

86
00:03:25,625 --> 00:03:26,802
Pause and think.

87
00:03:30,002 --> 00:03:30,902
Yes!

88
00:03:30,469 --> 00:03:33,577
Up there, gravity is almost
nine tenths as strong.

89
00:03:33,577 --> 00:03:37,812
They float because they and the
station fall together, round the Earth.

90
00:03:38,462 --> 00:03:40,197
Now, one worked example.

91
00:03:40,547 --> 00:03:42,993
A sixty kilogram man is in the lift.

92
00:03:42,993 --> 00:03:47,469
It speeds up going down, at one point
eight metres per second squared.

93
00:03:47,469 --> 00:03:49,209
What does the scale read?

94
00:03:49,709 --> 00:03:54,328
The acceleration points down,
so N equals m times g minus a.

95
00:03:55,078 --> 00:03:59,164
N is sixty, times nine point
eight minus one point eight.

96
00:03:59,164 --> 00:04:00,571
Sixty times eight.

97
00:04:00,571 --> 00:04:02,847
Four hundred and eighty newtons.

98
00:04:03,597 --> 00:04:05,515
The scale shows N over g.

99
00:04:05,515 --> 00:04:07,897
Four eighty over nine point eight.

100
00:04:07,897 --> 00:04:09,794
About forty nine kilograms.

101
00:04:10,544 --> 00:04:14,764
If the lift fell freely, a would be
g, and the scale would read zero.

102
00:04:15,414 --> 00:04:16,879
Now, the exam trap.

103
00:04:17,229 --> 00:04:21,431
Many students think going up means
heavier, and going down means lighter.

104
00:04:21,781 --> 00:04:23,791
But only the acceleration counts.

105
00:04:23,791 --> 00:04:26,650
Going up while slowing
down, you feel lighter.

106
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:28,760
Where is this asked?

107
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:32,231
In lift numericals, often
with a velocity time graph.

108
00:04:32,831 --> 00:04:34,021
Exam level now.

109
00:04:34,021 --> 00:04:37,410
A sixty kilogram man stands
on a scale in a lift.

110
00:04:37,410 --> 00:04:41,990
From rest, it speeds up to three
metres per second in two seconds going

111
00:04:41,990 --> 00:04:45,349
up, moves steadily, then
stops in three seconds.

112
00:04:45,349 --> 00:04:46,249
g is ten.

113
00:04:46,237 --> 00:04:50,756
Find the ratio of the largest to
the smallest reading while it moves.

114
00:04:50,756 --> 00:04:51,996
Pause and solve.

115
00:04:57,396 --> 00:04:58,707
The answer is A.

116
00:04:58,707 --> 00:05:00,476
Twenty three to eighteen.

117
00:05:00,976 --> 00:05:03,790
Speeding up, a is plus one point five.

118
00:05:03,790 --> 00:05:06,285
N is sixty times eleven point five.

119
00:05:06,285 --> 00:05:07,975
Six hundred and ninety.

120
00:05:08,475 --> 00:05:10,580
Stopping, a is minus one.

121
00:05:10,580 --> 00:05:12,408
N is sixty times nine.

122
00:05:12,408 --> 00:05:14,225
Five hundred and forty.

123
00:05:14,725 --> 00:05:18,528
Six ninety over five forty is
twenty three over eighteen.

124
00:05:19,128 --> 00:05:20,582
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:20,582 --> 00:05:22,077
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:25,277 --> 00:05:28,275
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

127
00:05:28,675 --> 00:05:29,575
One.

128
00:05:29,152 --> 00:05:31,360
What does a scale actually measure?

129
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:33,160
Two.

130
00:05:32,743 --> 00:05:34,893
The lift goes up and slows down.

131
00:05:34,893 --> 00:05:37,572
Do you feel heavier or lighter, and why?

132
00:05:38,472 --> 00:05:39,372
Three.

133
00:05:39,064 --> 00:05:43,193
Why do astronauts float, even
though gravity still pulls on them?

134
00:05:44,293 --> 00:05:46,064
Next time, a new chapter.

135
00:05:46,064 --> 00:05:48,757
Push a wall all day, and it never moves.

136
00:05:48,757 --> 00:05:50,202
Did you do any work?

137
00:05:50,202 --> 00:05:52,746
We find out in work, energy and power.
